        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>Orationes contra Arianos III.55</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Whom God hath raised up, having loosed the pains of death: because it was not possible that he should be holden of it&#x27; — Athanasius marks ouk ēn dynaton krateisthai auton hyp&#x27; autou (it was-not-possible that he be-held-by it) as the IMPOSSIBILITY-OF-DEATH-HOLDING-THE-WORD. Death cannot hold the deathless-Word; the assumed-flesh died, but the Word&#x27;s-presence-in-it ensured that death-itself was-defeated-from-within. The Petrine apostolic-kerygma at Pentecost (Acts 2:14-36) is itself an extended argument for the Word&#x27;s-divinity from the resurrection-FACT-and-its-impossibility-otherwise. Tewahedo Easter Trisagion-Paschal preaching cites Acts 2:24 + Ps 16:10 as the harrowing-Resurrection apostolic-pair.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, Orationes contra Arianos (Four Discourses Against the Arians, I-IV), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 26 (1857).",
