        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>Epistulae ad Serapionem 1.30</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;But he, being full of the Holy Ghost, looked up stedfastly into heaven, and saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing on the right hand of God&#x27; — Athanasius marks Stephanos plērēs Pneumatos Hagiou (Stephen full of the Holy Spirit) eiden doxan theou kai Iēsoun (saw glory-of-God and Jesus) as the TRINITARIAN-EYE-WITNESS martyrdom-scene: Stephen, IN-the-Spirit, sees the Father-glory + the Son-at-the-right-hand. All three divine Persons are simultaneously-participated-in the witness. The &#x27;standing&#x27; (hestōta) — distinct from the usual seated-at-right-hand — is the rare-Acts iconography of Christ-rising-to-receive-the-martyr.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, Epistulae ad Serapionem de Spiritu Sancto (Letters to Serapion on the Holy Spirit, I-IV), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 26 (1857).",
