        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>De Incarnatione Verbi §40</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And he commanded the chariot to stand still: and they went down both into the water, both Philip and the eunuch; and he baptized him&#x27; — Athanasius marks the ETHIOPIAN-EUNUCH&#x27;S BAPTISM as the DI-§40 canonical-anchor for the Word&#x27;s-Spirit-empowered missionary-extension to the ends-of-the-earth. The Ethiopian-eunuch is the first-named Gentile-baptismal-recipient in Acts (before Cornelius ch. 10) and the apostolic-tradition that founds Christianity in Aksumite-Tewahedo geography (per Eusebius Hist. Eccl. 2.1.13 + Tewahedo internal tradition). Athanasius — Frumentius&#x27;s consecrator (c. 330 AD) — was the patriarch who ordained the first Tewahedo bishop, making Acts 8:38 the foundational Athanasian-Tewahedo institutional anchor.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, De Incarnatione Verbi (On the Incarnation), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 25 (1857).",
